# xpkit: experimentation and causal inference you can defend

<!-- Generated by scripts/build_docs.py from results/**/*.json. Edit docs/templates/README.md.j2, not this file. -->

A tested Python toolkit and three real-data case studies. It covers checking whether an experiment can be trusted, measuring effects with honest uncertainty, estimating what is possible when you *can't* randomize, deciding who to target, and turning each result into a business decision. Every estimator is checked against a trusted reference implementation (scipy, statsmodels, published tables), a simulation with a known true effect, or both. Every number below is generated by code in this repo.

**Verified results**

- **Peeking control:** checking a test at 10 interim looks and stopping at the first p < 0.05 produced false positives in **{{ nul.naive_peeking.rejection_rate | pct(1) }}** of null experiments. The O'Brien-Fleming boundaries implemented here held it at **{{ nul.obrien_fleming.rejection_rate | pct(1) }}**, kept {{ alt.obrien_fleming.rejection_rate | pct(1) }} power (vs {{ alt.single_look.rejection_rate | pct(1) }} for a single look) and stopped at {{ alt.obrien_fleming.expected_sample_fraction | pct(0) }} of the planned sample on average ({{ "{:,}".format(s2.C_sequential.n_sims) }} simulations).
- **CUPED:** with a pre-period covariate correlated ρ = {{ "%.2f" | format(cs.rho_y_pre) }} with the outcome, variance fell by **{{ cs.empirical_variance_reduction_cuped | pct(0) }}** (theory: {{ cs.theoretical_variance_reduction | pct(0) }}) with no detectable bias ({{ '%+.4f' | format(cs.results.cuped.bias) }} ± {{ '%.4f' | format(cs.results.cuped.bias_mcse) }}) and {{ cs.results.cuped.coverage | pct(1) }} CI coverage.
- **Targeting (Criteo, {{ "{:,}".format(crit.split_sizes.test) }} held-out users):** targeting the top {{ cb.decision.chosen_depth | pct(0) }} by predicted uplift earns **{{ (cb.decision.test_profit_per_user * 1000) | money }} per 1,000 users** versus {{ (cb.decision.test_random_profit_per_user * 1000) | money }} for random targeting at the same depth, under stated cost assumptions.
- **Re-examining the original analysis (GA360):** traffic that looks internal (almost all of it one desktop-only referral segment) accounts for {{ ga.internal_traffic.share_of_desktop_conversions | pct(0) }} of desktop conversions. Excluding it shrinks the adjusted mobile-desktop gap from {{ gA.estimate | pp(2) }} to **{{ gX.estimate | pp(2) }}**. The original "mobile converts at a quarter of desktop" headline overstated the gap, which was observational to begin with.

